{
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "The reality-virtuality continuum is a scale that describes environments by their range between real and virtual content.",
      "sentence_ids": [113, 116, 128]
    },
    {
      "text": "A real environment is one extreme of the reality-virtuality continuum and contains no virtual content.",
      "sentence_ids": [117, 119, 129]
    },
    {
      "text": "A virtual environment is the other extreme of the reality-virtuality continuum and contains no real content.",
      "sentence_ids": [118, 119, 129]
    },
    {
      "text": "Augmented reality occurs when virtual or digital content is added to the real world, thereby augmenting the real environment.",
      "sentence_ids": [120, 122, 130]
    },
    {
      "text": "Augmented virtuality occurs when real-world content is added to a virtual environment, thereby augmenting the virtual environment.",
      "sentence_ids": [120, 121, 123, 130]
    },
    {
      "text": "On the reality-virtuality continuum, augmented reality lies toward the real-environment side of the intermediate region, while augmented virtuality lies toward the virtual-environment side.",
      "sentence_ids": [120, 126]
    },
    {
      "text": "Displaying a real human figure inside a virtual environment is an example of augmented virtuality.",
      "sentence_ids": [124]
    },
    {
      "text": "Displaying a digital character in a real-world setting is an example of augmented reality.",
      "sentence_ids": [125]
    },
    {
      "text": "The intermediate region of the continuum includes varying degrees of real environments augmented with virtual content and virtual environments augmented with real content.",
      "sentence_ids": [130]
    },
    {
      "text": "In a broad sense, the entire space between the real and virtual extremes is called mixed reality.",
      "sentence_ids": [126, 130]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "XR stands for Extended Reality.",
      "sentence_ids": [84]
    },
    {
      "text": "AR, VR, and MR stand for Augmented Reality, Virtual Reality, and Mixed Reality, respectively.",
      "sentence_ids": [83]
    },
    {
      "text": "XR is an umbrella term rather than a specific technology.",
      "sentence_ids": [85, 88, 89]
    },
    {
      "text": "XR includes all present or future immersive technologies.",
      "sentence_ids": [86, 101, 110]
    },
    {
      "text": "AR, VR, and MR are examples of immersive technologies included under XR.",
      "sentence_ids": [85, 88, 111]
    },
    {
      "text": "The X in XR can be understood either as the initial letter of \"extended\" or as a variable.",
      "sentence_ids": [93, 94]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treating X as a variable, substituting A, V, or M produces the technology names AR, VR, and MR.",
      "sentence_ids": [95, 96, 98, 99]
    },
    {
      "text": "The variable X can also take other values, and each value can correspond to a present or future immersive technology.",
      "sentence_ids": [97, 100]
    },
    {
      "text": "An immersive technology creates an environment that is perceived through sensory organs as real, causing users to fail to distinguish it from the real environment.",
      "sentence_ids": [104, 106, 107, 108]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "In everyday use, the terms AR, VR, MR, and XR are not interpreted identically by all people.",
      "sentence_ids": [43]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some people associate AR, VR, MR, or XR with fantastical, magical, or imaginary movie environments, such as scenes in films like Avatar.",
      "sentence_ids": [45, 46, 47]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some people associate these terms with video games, which they view as imaginary but highly interactive worlds.",
      "sentence_ids": [49]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some people consider 3D or 4D movie-theater documentaries, such as documentaries about extinct animals, to be examples of AR or VR.",
      "sentence_ids": [52, 53, 56]
    },
    {
      "text": "A common perception of VR is that a user wears a headset that blocks ordinary vision and allows the user to experience a virtual world.",
      "sentence_ids": [58, 59, 60]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some people associate AR with mobile games such as Pokémon Go, in which a virtual object appears on a device screen over a view of the real world even though the object is not physically present.",
      "sentence_ids": [61, 62, 63, 64]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some people associate AR or VR with visualizations that reveal hidden internal structures, such as the skeletal structure or vein network of a leg instead of only the skin.",
      "sentence_ids": [69, 70, 71, 73]
    },
    {
      "text": "Common associations with AR, VR, MR, and XR do not by themselves establish which media or applications actually belong to those technologies.",
      "sentence_ids": [48, 50, 51, 54, 57, 65, 66, 67, 68, 74, 75, 76
]
}
    ]
  }
